35,2,1,talk,May,,"It's interesting; the late-evening pilot was a success and the contactless trial worked well. Also, past lift and entrance rebuilds were completed on time, but lane-priority stretches didn't last. History suggests some paths are safer than others.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"My findings are concerning. The late-turn pool is severely thinned, and roadway crews are a third below establishment. However, the lift and escalator maintenance contract actually has spare capacity. We must be careful not to overstretch our people.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Midori,,"My findings show that mid-city surface runs are taking much longer than they used to, yet crowds clear at the same rate. Also, journey audits suggest passengers lose most time waiting for connections, not at ticketing machines.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"I have found that work inside pre-war station shells is quite risky, with unexpected voids causing long delays. Also, it seems the ferry and rail offices haven't agreed on data terms, though roadway consents are already settled.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"My findings suggest a tight financial situation. The technology and operating subsidies have been trimmed, and ticketing contractor fees are now exceeding our resurfacing budget. We must be mindful of long-term costs.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,May,,"@Benjamin, it sounds like we have a real staffing crisis for road-based projects, which aligns with my finding that lane-priority stretches failed. Perhaps we should avoid plans that rely heavily on roadway crews?","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@May, your observation is astute. Given the deficit in roadway and signals crews, any plan requiring significant road-based labor or enforcement is likely to fail or cause operational collapse. We should prioritize options with available support.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Benjamin, @May, that is a very important point. If we look at the passenger experience, the mid-city delays are significant, but we must balance that against the actual capacity to implement a solution.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"It is a bit worrying to hear about the staffing, isn't it? I also found that working in the older station shells is quite unpredictable, which might make those projects very difficult to finish on time.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Shizue, that unpredictability is concerning. Combined with the fact that our technology and operating subsidies are being trimmed, we should be very cautious about any plan that risks significant cost overruns or high ongoing fees.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,May,,"@Jonathan, if we avoid high ongoing fees and @Benjamin's staffing gaps, we might be leaning away from ticketing overhauls and road corridors. @Midori, does that leave us with options that actually help passengers?","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Jonathan"",""Benjamin"",""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Midori, indeed. If we avoid the roadway staffing deficit and the financial risks @Jonathan mentioned, we are left with options that utilize existing maintenance capacity or proven historical successes. Logic suggests we focus there.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Midori"",""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Midori,,"It's a difficult balance. While mid-city delays are real, the journey audits show that connection times are the bigger issue. If we avoid road-based projects and high fees, we should look for a plan that improves flow.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"I agree with Midori. If we avoid the risks of the pre-war shells and the disagreements between the ferry and rail offices, we can find a path that is peaceful and practical for everyone.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree. We should seek a solution that avoids the volatility of pre-war construction and the burden of excessive contractor fees, ensuring we don't commit to a financial drain we cannot sustain.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,May,,"It seems we've narrowed it down. We need something that avoids roadway staffing gaps, pre-war shell risks, and high contractor fees, while ideally building on a proven success. I'm curious to see the candidates now.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"I agree. We have established a clear set of constraints: avoid roadway labor, minimize financial volatility, and steer clear of unpredictable structural risks. I look forward to seeing which candidate fits these criteria.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Midori,,"I hope the candidates offer a solution that addresses the connection delays I found, while remaining feasible given the staffing and budget constraints we've discussed. I'm ready to see the options.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Shizue,,I agree with you all. It is so heartening that we have looked at this from every side. I am ready to see the candidates and help choose the most harmonious path forward.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,I agree. We have a clear framework for our decision. I am ready to review the candidates and ensure we select a plan that is fiscally responsible and sustainable for the long term.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
