60,3,1,talk,May,,"Looking at the options, A is risky due to the data disagreements Shizue found and the fees Jonathan mentioned. B fails because of the roadway staffing gaps. C is risky with those pre-war shells. D seems the safest.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Shizue"",""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"@May, I must disagree on D. My research showed the late-turn pool is severely thinned; extending night services would push an exhausted staff to a breaking point. C is the only logical choice given the spare maintenance capacity.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Midori,,"@Benjamin, I'm worried about C because of the pre-war shell risks Shizue mentioned. However, my audits show connection times are the real issue. If C improves flow between platforms and streets, it might be the most helpful.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"@Benjamin, I am very concerned about the pre-war shells. The voids could cause long delays and stress for everyone. But @May, if the night service is too hard on the staff, perhaps C is our best hope.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"@May, @Benjamin, I must caution against A and D. A risks high contractor fees and budget cuts, while D's staffing needs are unsustainable. C is risky, but B's road costs are climbing. C seems the most viable.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""May"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,May,,"@Benjamin, I hadn't considered the late-turn pool's exhaustion for D. If D is out and B is impossible, we are left with A or C. But A's data conflicts make it a nightmare. Let's go with C.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@May, I appreciate your flexibility. While the pre-war shells are a risk, the spare capacity in the lift and escalator maintenance contract provides a logical safety net that the other options simply lack. I vote for C.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Midori,,"@Benjamin, @May, I agree. While the structural risks are worrying, the other options conflict too strongly with our staffing and budget constraints. Improving station flow addresses passenger time and seems the most balanced choice. I vote for C.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"I feel a bit uneasy about the risks in the old shells, but I trust your reasoning. If it is the most harmonious choice for our staff and budget, I shall support it. I vote for C.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree. While the structural risks are present, the other options present far more certain failures in staffing and finance. C is the most sustainable path forward. I vote for C.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,May,,I'm glad we've reached a consensus. It's a relief to have a clear direction that respects both our history and our current constraints. I vote for C.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"Since we have all reached a consensus based on the available data and logical constraints, I see no reason to deviate. My vote remains firmly for C.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":true,""norm"":[{""code"":""trailing_over"",""token"":""Over"",""tail"":""Over.""}]}"
